The other rule I settled on: abstract subjects only. Sailboats, paths through grass, kites with cut strings. Think about the feeling of the post, not its subject matter. "A magnifying glass over scattered dots" works better for a search post than "a search results page." FLUX.2-dev can't render text anyway, which honestly helps -- no temptation to put words on the image.

## Semi-automated

I wrote a [Node script](https://github.com/khawkins98/allaboutken-11ty/blob/main/scripts/image-generate/generate-image.mjs) that reads a post's frontmatter, guesses whether it's technical/impact/opinion, and picks prompts from a small library. It fires off three generations in parallel -- about 30 seconds -- then opens them all in Preview.

```
$ yarn generate-image src/site/posts/20260303-generating-blog-art.njk

Generating 3 images for "Penny-a-piece blog art" (technical)...
```

Pick a number and the script copies a frontmatter snippet to your clipboard. It offers to clean up the unchosen files. I usually crop a bit and nudge the saturation before committing -- Eleventy handles the responsive formats at build time so there's nothing else to fuss with.

There's also a [bash script](https://github.com/khawkins98/allaboutken-11ty/blob/main/scripts/image-generate/generate-image.sh) for when you already know the prompt. Both read from the same config.

Three images per post runs about $0.03. [Scripts are on GitHub](https://github.com/khawkins98/allaboutken-11ty/tree/main/scripts/image-generate) if you want to steal the approach.

